#### Direction-pooling sensitivity (`build_dirs.py`)

The starter pipeline computes the refusal direction from the **last prompt token's activation only**. This is defensible for *classification* — the last-token activation is what the model uses to decide what to generate next — but may be too narrow for ablation. We re-computed the direction three ways using 845 cached full-token residuals at layers `[8, 16, 32, 48, 56]` (~50/50 class split):

- **last-token at layer 48** (matches starter pipeline math, single layer)
- **mean-pool across all prompt tokens at layer 48** (single layer)
- **mean-pool at all 5 cached layers** (multi-layer)

| Variant | cos with last-tok | Behavior flip rate |
|---|---|---|
| last_tok L48 (single) | 1.000 | [PENDING] |
| meanpool L48 (single) | __ | [PENDING] |
| meanpool multilayer | __ | [PENDING] |

If the mean-pool direction shows substantially higher flip rate, the last-token bias was the bottleneck. If it doesn't, the finding is robust: **single-direction-based ablation does not control Gemma 4-31B's refusal behavior, regardless of pooling.**

