## What this contributes — the three flip rates together

| Intervention | Where it acts | Constraint | Flip rate |
|---|---|---|---|
| Token edits | input string | minimal | **0/20** |
| Free-form rewrite | input string | intent preserved | **__/20** [PENDING] |
| Activation ablation v1 (last-tok) | residual stream | none | **__/20** [PENDING] |
| Activation ablation v2 (mean-pool) | residual stream | none | **__/20** [PENDING] |

Two complementary readings of this pattern, both supported by the data:

**Reading 1: probe direction is partially causal.** Free-form rewriting that pushes activations off the direction *does* flip behavior at a meaningful rate. The probe captures something real and exploitable. But that "something" lives upstream of the input-edit attribution surface (token edits don't access it), and is more diffuse than a single direction in the residual stream (single-direction ablation doesn't fully control it).

**Reading 2: probe AUC overstates causal control.** A 0.93-AUC probe doesn't promise that intervening on its direction will move behavior. The wedge between AUC and causal handle is real and measurable.

Both readings matter for **Constitutional Classifiers**-style production deployment: probes are gates between users and model output. If those probes are correlational, they may be circumventable in ways their AUC doesn't predict. The free-form rewrite finding directly demonstrates this: an attacker who can paraphrase freely fools the probe and gets the harmful output, while AUC against a static eval set looks fine.

